<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :contributing %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:contributing) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">

      <!-- Run it locally -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Run It Locally') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            You need Ruby (see <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.ruby-version</code>) and Node (see the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">engines</code> field in <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">package.json</code>). Then run two commands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
          language: 'bash',
          demo_code: 'bin/setup   # gems, npm, token generation, and every CSS build
bin/dev     # server on :3001 with live Sass and Tailwind rebuilds'
        ) %>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          Open <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">http://localhost:3001/framework</code> to view the docs. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">server/app/assets/builds/</code> is gitignored, so re-run <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bin/setup</code> after a pull if the CSS looks stale.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Where things live -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Where Things Live') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The framework and its docs share one Rails app. These are the paths you will touch most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">app/assets/stylesheets/framework/</code>: the Sass design system (base, components, utilities, themes, mixins).</li>
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">app/javascript/</code>: the JavaScript runtime (terminalize, TRMNLPaint, TRMNLCharts) and the docs harness.</li>
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">app/views/framework/*.html.erb</code>: one docs page per framework feature.</li>
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">app/helpers/framework_helper.rb</code>: page intro paragraphs and docs helpers.</li>
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">db/data/</code>: the token and release data (colors, fonts, devices, versions).</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          The full directory map is in <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">README.md</code>. For what this repository is and the rule that shapes it, see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :open_source) %>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Test suites -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The Test Suites') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Run the layer that matches your change, or run all of them before opening a pull request.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">npm run test:stylesheets</code>: public classes, variables, theme contracts, and the CSS paint data.</li>
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">npm run test:scripts</code>: runtime syntax, the public API names, and the CSS to JavaScript paint boundary.</li>
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bundle exec rspec</code>: the Rails docs routes, versioned bundle selection, and generated endpoints.</li>
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">npm run test:runtime</code>: terminalize, the layout engines, and the paint API in Chromium.</li>
          <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">npm run test:visual</code>: rendered CSS against platform-specific PNG baselines.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          The last two use Playwright. Install its browser once per machine.
        </p>

        <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
          language: 'bash',
          demo_code: 'npx playwright install chromium'
        ) %>

        <div class="<%= message_sage_classes %>">
          <div class="flex items-center w-full">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0 items-center">
              <%= render 'shared/icons/info' %>
            </div>
            <div class="w-full flex items-center justify-between ml-3">
              <p class="leading-none text-sm font-medium">
                The runtime and visual suites run entirely on your machine. Both also run in CI: Linux Chromium against committed Linux baselines for visuals, and the same runtime checks as a local run.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Writing docs -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Writing Documentation') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The docs are the framework's test bench, so most changes touch a docs page. The copy follows a house style.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li>Lead with what the reader can do or what the thing is.</li>
          <li>Keep paragraphs to one to three short sentences, one idea each.</li>
          <li>Never use em-dashes. Rewrite with a period, comma, colon, or parentheses.</li>
          <li>Inside a <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.screen</code> demo, use framework classes only (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">description</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">label</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">value</code>).</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          The full guide is the write-docs skill under <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.claude/skills/write-docs/</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Good PR -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'What Makes a Good PR') %>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li>Keep it small and focused. One concern per pull request.</li>
          <li>Change a utility? Update its docs page and its live example in the same change. A utility without a rendered example is incomplete.</li>
          <li>Run the test suites and confirm <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bin/build</code> succeeds before you push.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          Releases are maintainer-only. Land your change, and a maintainer cuts the release. See <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">CONTRIBUTING.md</code> for the rest.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Issues and security -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Report Issues and Security') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Found a bug? Open a GitHub issue with a minimal reproduction. Ideas and feature requests are welcome as issues too.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Security issues are different. Report them through GitHub's private vulnerability reporting, not a public issue. See <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">SECURITY.md</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            All participation follows the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
